<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="vitamin E biosynthesis (tocopherols)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-1422" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The term |FRAME: Vitamin-E| (tocochromenols) is a collective term for the eight forms of |FRAME: Tocotrienols tocotrienols| and |FRAME: Tocopherols tocopherols|, each existing as &amp;alpha;, &amp;beta;, &amp;gamma;, and &amp;delta; species. Structurally tocotrienols and tocopherols differ only in the degree of unsaturation of their side-chain. The tocotrienol chain comprises three &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;-double bonds, whereas the tocopherol chain is fully saturated. In addition, the &amp;alpha;, &amp;beta;, &amp;gamma;, and &amp;delta; species of both compound groups vary in the methylation pattern of the chromanol ring |CITS:[12897790]|. Vitamin E compounds are synthesized only by photosynthetic organisms, although they are essential dietary components for animals (including humans), where they act primarily as antioxidants. Their presumed role in plants involve protection of chloroplast lipids and chlorophylls against oxidative damages. The tocotrienols are mostly found in monocots |CITS:[Padley94]| and a few dicots |CITS:[17110122]|, whereas tocopherols are wider spread in the plant kingdom and dominate in the dicots |CITS:[8827691]|. The |FRAME: Tocopherols tocopherols| include |FRAME: ALPHA-TOCOPHEROL|, |FRAME: BETA-TOCOPHEROL|, |FRAME: GAMA-TOCOPHEROL| and |FRAME:DELTA-TOCOPHEROL|, which differ only in the number and position of methyl groups on the chromanol ring. |FRAME: ALPHA-TOCOPHEROL| has been shown to possess the highest vitamin E activity. The genes encoding all the of pathway enzymes have been cloned from Arabidopsis.</Description>
  <Comment source="Automated Conversion">This GPML file was automatically generated from PlantCyc flatfiles.</Comment>
